WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.080
Were these actors not bold enough&nbsp;
to go where no one has gone before?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.100
Or were there other factors behind&nbsp;
their refusal of "Star Trek" roles?

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.180
As the human-Vulcan first officer of the&nbsp;
Enterprise and an instant favorite among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:17.400
the fanbase, Leonard Nimoy's Spock is one of the&nbsp;
most iconic characters on television. While it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:22.140
may be hard to imagine anyone but Nimoy playing&nbsp;
Spock on "Star Trek: The Original Series," the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:27.060
actor was reportedly not series creator Gene&nbsp;
Roddenberry's first pick for the part. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:31.260
Roddenberry favored comedic actor George&nbsp;
Lindsey, best known for playing the goofy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:35.040
Goober Pyle on "The Andy Griffith Show"&nbsp;
and its sequel series "Mayberry R.F.D."

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:40.740
In a short television bumper for TVLand, Nimoy&nbsp;
attests that Lindsey was Roddenberry's first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:45.720
choice for the role that made him a household&nbsp;
name. This claim is corroborated by prolific actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:49.140
Ernest Borgnine in his memoir "Ernie,"&nbsp;
recalling that his friend Lindsey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:52.560
turned the part of Spock down, without&nbsp;
elaborating on the rationale behind the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.280
refusal. Lindsey would continue to&nbsp;
play largely comedic roles on film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:00.060
and television for the remainder of&nbsp;
his career before his death in 2012.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:04.920
Another actor reportedly offered the role&nbsp;
of Spock ahead of Nimoy was Academy Award&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:08.760
winner Martin Landau, who had appeared in&nbsp;
high-profile supporting roles in films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:13.560
including "North by Northwest." In a&nbsp;
1986 interview with Starlog Magazine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:17.880
Landau confirmed he was approached to take the&nbsp;
role of the Vulcan science officer but claimed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:22.440
he wasn't interested in playing an emotionless&nbsp;
character, unfavorably comparing Spock's stoic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:27.420
nature to a glorified newscaster. Instead,&nbsp;
he took on a very different television role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.800
starring as master of disguise Rollin Hand in&nbsp;
the original "Mission: Impossible" TV series.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:36.060
In a separate interview for "Where No Man&nbsp;
Has Gone Before: A History in Pictures,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:40.020
Roddenberry confirmed Landau was being&nbsp;
considered for the role, saying the actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:45.060
was a possibility. Elaborating on the potential&nbsp;
casting, Roddenberry attributed Landau's busy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:49.500
schedule with other projects at the time as the&nbsp;
primary cause behind his turning down the role.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:54.720
In a coincidental twist, Nimoy replaced Landau&nbsp;
on "Mission: Impossible" as the magician Paris&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:59.880
after Landau left the series in 1969 — several&nbsp;
months after the cancellation of "Star Trek."

00:02:00.272 --> 00:02:01.734
"Fascinating."

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:06.360
While developing "Star Trek IV: The Voyage&nbsp;
Home," director and co-writer Leonard Nimoy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:10.560
learned that popular comedian and actor Eddie&nbsp;
Murphy was apparently a big fan of the franchise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.560
In a retrospective interview&nbsp;
with The Hollywood Reporter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:17.880
co-writer Steve Meerson recalled that an early&nbsp;
script contained a part specifically written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:22.200
for Murphy, which would have had the comic&nbsp;
powerhouse interact with the Enterprise crew.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:26.520
Murphy was set to play an astrophysicist at&nbsp;
Berkeley while the "Original Series" crew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:32.084
visited California's Bay Area in 1986 to save&nbsp;
their timeline by rescuing humpback whales.

00:02:32.084 --> 00:02:36.960
"No, I wanna go, I wanna beam up and, you know,&nbsp;
be on the ship, you know, so I didn't do it."

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:38.927
"Yeah no, you have to be on the Enterprise."

00:02:38.927 --> 00:02:42.600
Negotiations with Murphy reportedly&nbsp;
broke down before filming began, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:47.700
leading the comedian/actor to drop out from the&nbsp;
project. His planned astrophysicist character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.660
was cut from the story entirely and replaced&nbsp;
with marine biologist Dr. Gillian Taylor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:55.440
played by Catherine Hicks. This&nbsp;
provided Kirk with a romantic foil.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:00.900
Acclaimed actor Edward James Olmos had two close&nbsp;
opportunities to be cast in major "Star Trek"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:05.400
roles, though he ultimately has yet to appear&nbsp;
in any iteration of the franchise. Director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:09.420
Leonard Nimoy approached Olmos to play the&nbsp;
villainous Commander Kruge in "Star Trek III:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:12.240
The Search for Spock" but was&nbsp;
overruled by the producers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:16.320
according to William Shatner's memoir&nbsp;
"Star Trek Movie Memories." However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:20.700
Olmos' second brush with "Star Trek" saw him&nbsp;
turn down an even bigger opportunity with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:25.000
the long-running sci-fi franchise that would&nbsp;
have made him an esteemed Starfleet captain.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:29.160
According to TrekMovie.com, he was&nbsp;
offered to star as Picard in "Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:32.520
The Next Generation," reportedly turning&nbsp;
down the part because of its high level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:36.780
of commitment and his own schedule at the&nbsp;
time. In an interview with The A.V. Club,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:41.580
Olmos explained that his decision to return to&nbsp;
sci-fi in "Battlestar Galactica" over his previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:45.960
"Star Trek" offer was driven by the quality of&nbsp;
storytelling present in the series' writing.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.060
The late celebrated actor Yaphet Kotto has&nbsp;
starred in a number of fan-favorite projects in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:55.560
film and television, including a part as Nostromo&nbsp;
technician Dennis Parker in the classic science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:00.720
fiction horror film "Alien." Kotto nearly took&nbsp;
on an even more iconic sci-fi role as Captain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:06.360
Jean-Luc Picard, the lead character in "Star Trek:&nbsp;
The Next Generation." According to a BBC interview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:11.340
with producer Robert Justman, series creator&nbsp;
Gene Roddenberry was initially not enthused by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:15.060
Patrick Stewart's audition for the part and&nbsp;
looked to other actors to take on the role.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:18.720
Among the actors who read for the&nbsp;
character was Kotto, who turned down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:23.340
the part over his concerns about shifting to&nbsp;
television following a prolific film career.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:27.060
In an interview with The Big Issue, Kotto&nbsp;
reflected on this decision with regret,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:31.920
referring to his rejection of the chance to&nbsp;
play Picard as a "wrong decision." Ironically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:36.780
he would transition to a television career years&nbsp;
later, starring as Baltimore police detective Al&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:41.520
Giardello across the entire run of the acclaimed&nbsp;
crime series "Homicide: Life on the Street."

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:48.180
The 1989 film "Star Trek V: The Final Frontier"&nbsp;
introduces Spock's long-lost older brother Sybok,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:51.646
who commandeers the Enterprise to&nbsp;
find God at the center of the galaxy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.640
Played by Laurence Luckinbill, Sybok is&nbsp;
a Vulcan in touch with and open about his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:01.140
emotions. The character was written for Sean&nbsp;
Connery, fresh off of his Academy Award-winning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:05.000
performance in "The Untouchables," but&nbsp;
the Scottish actor turned down the part.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:09.780
According to TrekMovie.com, Connery rejected the&nbsp;
role due to his commitments to appear in "Indiana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:14.580
Jones and the Last Crusade." Despite Connery's&nbsp;
absence in "The Final Frontier," the film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:19.800
holy planet Sha Ka Ree was named after him as a&nbsp;
subtle nod to the intended casting. Years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:24.000
it was revealed that a different actor, very&nbsp;
familiar to Trekkies, apparently vied for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:29.760
Sybok role unsuccessfully. In an interview with&nbsp;
StarTrek.com, Luckinbill claims that Nimoy lobbied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:34.885
to play Sybok for a dual role in "Star Trek V" but&nbsp;
was rejected because the siblings weren't twins.

00:05:34.885 --> 00:05:38.701
"Excuse me, I find that&nbsp;
selection highly illogical."

00:05:38.701 --> 00:05:42.900
As "Star Trek: The Next Generation" steadily&nbsp;
grew in popularity across its seven-season run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:47.880
the writers wanted to find a way to include actor&nbsp;
and comedian Robin Williams in a guest appearance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:52.020
Williams was a self-professed fan of "Star&nbsp;
Trek," even going so far as to reference the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:56.820
franchise directly in his hit sci-fi sitcom&nbsp;
"Mork and Mindy." The writers created a part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.820 --> 00:06:01.440
specifically for Williams in the Season 5 episode&nbsp;
"A Matter of Time" as Berlinghoff Rasmussen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:05.734
a time-traveling conman scheming to&nbsp;
steal Starfleet technology for himself.

00:06:05.734 --> 00:06:10.440
Unfortunately, scheduling conflicted with&nbsp;
the 1991 Steven Spielberg film "Hook," which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:14.640
prevented him from appearing on "The Next&nbsp;
Generation." Rasmussen was instead played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:19.246
by actor Matt Frewer, best known for his&nbsp;
'80s cult classic character Max Headroom.

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:23.580
Kim Cattrall appeared in "Star Trek VI:&nbsp;
The Undiscovered Country" as Valeris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:26.940
Spock's young Vulcan protege on the&nbsp;
Enterprise and the starship's new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:31.500
helmsman after Sulu is given his own command.&nbsp;
Though Valeris is eventually revealed to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:35.220
a traitor and part of the conspiracy to&nbsp;
assassinate Klingon chancellor Gowron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:39.480
Cattrall nearly had a continuing future with&nbsp;
the wider "Star Trek" franchise on television.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:44.460
However, rather than reprising her role as&nbsp;
the duplicitous Vulcan Valeris, Cattrall was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:48.780
offered the part of Bajoran security officer&nbsp;
Kira Nerys in "Star Trek: Deep Space Nine."

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:53.460
In a leaked casting memo for "Deep Space&nbsp;
Nine" from 1992, Cattrall is among the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:58.200
names appearing near the top of the list to play&nbsp;
the no-nonsense Bajoran freedom fighter. She is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:02.400
cited in the memo as passing on the role, though&nbsp;
no further explanation behind her decision is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:06.780
included in the document. Cattrall would find&nbsp;
considerable success elsewhere on television,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:10.920
starring as the vivacious businesswoman&nbsp;
and socialite Samantha Jones — a role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:15.401
she also nearly turned down — in the&nbsp;
acclaimed HBO series "Sex and the City."

00:07:15.401 --> 00:07:19.560
There are plenty of other names on the leaked&nbsp;
casting memo for Kira in "Deep Space Nine," all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:24.660
of whom are listed as a pass or unavailable. Next&nbsp;
to Cattrall, the most notable actor on the list&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:29.580
is Rita Wilson — prolific television and film&nbsp;
actor, and wife of lifelong "Star Trek" fan Tom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:34.500
Hanks. However, like Cattrall and the other actors&nbsp;
under consideration, Wilson turned down the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:38.160
with the memo citing she wasn't interested&nbsp;
in working in television at the time.

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:43.080
Interestingly, Nana Visitor, who ultimately was&nbsp;
cast as Kira for "Deep Space Nine" and "Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:48.240
Lower Decks" is not among the names listed on the&nbsp;
memo. Visitor's agent cautioned her against taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:52.140
the role, feeling it would be detrimental&nbsp;
to her career. But as Variety explains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:56.700
she was impressed by the character and accepted.&nbsp;
Wilson has gone on to play numerous supporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.700 --> 00:08:01.649
roles in film and television, notably as recurring&nbsp;
characters on "The Good Wife" and "Girls."

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:05.700
While Kate Mulgrew may be best known for&nbsp;
bringing Kathryn Janeway to life in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:10.020
television shows "Star Trek: Voyager" and "Star&nbsp;
Trek: Prodigy," she was not the first actor cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:14.520
in the role. Academy Award-nominated actor&nbsp;
Geneviève Bujold was initially cast as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:18.420
"Voyager" commanding officer, Captain Nicole&nbsp;
Janeway, maintaining the role as principal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:23.640
photography began in 1994. However, as The&nbsp;
New York Times reports, two days into filming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:27.660
Bujold decided to quit the series over concerns&nbsp;
regarding the show's demanding work schedule.

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:31.140
"Keeping me awake for four days&nbsp;
straight with the constant pain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:33.120
of your devices drilling into my skull."

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:36.420
Mulgrew, who was among the actors that&nbsp;
initially read for the role of Janeway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:40.320
during the casting process, was called back&nbsp;
in the wake of Bujold's sudden departure and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:45.240
accepted the lead part. With the character renamed&nbsp;
Kathryn Janeway to reflect the shift in actors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:49.740
Mulgrew quickly brought the memorable captain&nbsp;
to life as filming resumed. For her part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:53.580
Bujold returned to focus primarily on&nbsp;
film work with the occasional TV movie;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:57.600
she never starred as the lead in a television&nbsp;
series across her distinguished career.

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:03.120
Audiences were treated to a bold reinvention of&nbsp;
the "Star Trek" franchise in 2009, with filmmaker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:07.920
J.J. Abrams helming a reboot movie that recast&nbsp;
the classic characters from "The Original Series."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:12.180
Among the iconic crew reimagined for the&nbsp;
film was Spock, played by Zachary Quinto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:16.740
across the trilogy as a Vulcan with a fiery&nbsp;
temper under his stoic exterior. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:21.480
Quinto was not the only actor in consideration&nbsp;
for the role; Academy Award winner Adrien&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:25.980
Brody was approached by Abrams to potentially&nbsp;
portray the Enterprise's logical first officer.

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:30.840
In an interview with MTV, Brody confirmed&nbsp;
he spoke with Abrams about playing Spock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:34.740
in the 2009 "Star Trek," even sharing&nbsp;
the news of his potential casting with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:37.980
his parents. Though he expressed&nbsp;
regret about not taking the part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:42.661
he has not fully divulged specifics as to why&nbsp;
he didn't appear as the iconic science officer.

00:09:42.926 --> 00:09:47.280
With a new, younger cast of the "Original&nbsp;
Series" era front and center in the 2009&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:52.500
"Star Trek," one of the actors widely rumored to&nbsp;
be considered to play James Kirk was Matt Damon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:57.000
Damon himself publicly dismissed the casting&nbsp;
as internet rumors, observing that the reboot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:01.200
was looking for a younger actor for the role.&nbsp;
However, months after the movie was released,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:04.740
it was revealed that Damon was being&nbsp;
considered for a different character.

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:09.060
Abrams confirmed that he did approach Damon&nbsp;
for a role in the film, later clarifying in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:12.180
an interview with MTV that he spoke to&nbsp;
him about playing James Kirk's father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:15.960
George. Abrams revealed that Damon&nbsp;
graciously turned down the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:19.620
so Australian actor Chris Hemsworth&nbsp;
played the ill-fated George Kirk instead.

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:25.500
2013's "Star Trek Into Darkness" is a strange&nbsp;
sequel, offering a loose retread of "Star Trek II:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:29.400
The Wrath of Khan" within the storytelling&nbsp;
sensibilities of the reboot timeline that began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:35.040
in 2009. The film pits the Enterprise crew against&nbsp;
the genetically enhanced superhuman Khan Noonien&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:40.740
Singh — the classic villainous role originated by&nbsp;
Ricardo Montalban. Academy Award winner Benicio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:45.360
del Toro entered negotiations to play the reboot&nbsp;
timeline's iteration of Khan during a time when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:50.501
the identity of the movie's antagonist remained&nbsp;
unconfirmed in the face of mounting speculation.

00:10:50.501 --> 00:10:54.240
According to Vulture, Del Toro decided to&nbsp;
drop out of "Into Darkness" after salary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:59.940
negotiations broke down — approximately one month&nbsp;
before filming was scheduled to begin. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:04.920
English actor Benedict Cumberbatch played Khan in&nbsp;
the final film. "Into Darkness" endured criticism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:09.540
for casting a Caucasian actor as Khan, while&nbsp;
director J.J. Abrams admitted attempting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:13.920
keep Khan's true identity a secret was&nbsp;
a mistake. Del Toro would go on to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:18.360
two very different sci-fi roles in "Star Wars:&nbsp;
The Last Jedi" and "Guardians of the Galaxy."

